<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henri Becquerel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1852">1852</a>–<a href="/years/1908">1908</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Antoine Henri Becquerel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antoine Henri Becquerel was a French experimental physicist, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> into a family of physicists, who discovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radioactivity">radioactivity</a>. While investigating phosphorescence after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-rontgen">Wilhelm Röntgen</a>'s discovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/x-ray">X-rays</a>, he found in early 1896 that uranium salts left in a dark cabinet still exposed photographic plates, and by May he concluded that the radiation came from the uranium itself. He shared the 1903 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physics with Marie and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-curie">Pierre Curie</a>, and also identified beta particles as high-speed electrons. He died at Le Croisic in 1908, aged 55, with serious burns on his skin reported, likely from radioactive materials. The SI unit of radioactivity is named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henri-becquerel.jpg" alt="Henri Becquerel" width="330" height="473" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul Nadar / Adam Cuerden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paul_Nadar_-_Henri_Becquerel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
